THE SAME BRANCH BYTES ACQUIRE A CONDITION FROM IF-THEN STATE.

English Major C bottom-up machine-byte specimen.
Status: an actually assembled/disassembled ARMv7-A T32
diagnostic and two stated hypothetical architectural
machine-state traces. NOT real ARM device execution,
and NOT a functioning English Major C compiler.

FOREIGN BYTE PROVENANCE.

The following literal instruction halfwords were placed in
one temporary Thumb instruction stream in a diagnostic
Linux x86-64 environment:

  .syntax unified
  .arch armv7-a
  .thumb
  .text
  .global branch_inside_it
  .thumb_func
  branch_inside_it:
  .hword 0x2800,0xBF08,0xE000,0x2103,0x2204,0xDF03

Clang 17.0.0, targeting armv7a-none-eabi in Thumb state,
produced an ELF32 little-endian ARM object. LLVM objdump
17.0.0, decoding under thumbv7-none-eabi, displayed:

  offset 0x0000  halfword 2800  cmp   r0,#0
  offset 0x0002  halfword BF08  it    eq
  offset 0x0004  halfword E000  beq   0x0008
  offset 0x0006  halfword 2103  movs  r1,#3
  offset 0x0008  halfword 2204  movs  r2,#4
  offset 0x000A  halfword DF03  svc   #3

The apparent "beq" at offset 0x0004 matters. The RAW
HALFWORD IS E000. Its top five bits are 11100.
It is the T2 unconditional immediate B encoding,
not the T1 condition-coded branch encoding D0xx.
The effective EQ condition comes from the preceding
IT EQ state, not from any condition-code bit field
inside halfword E000.

A disassembly printed without the surrounding IT
context may call exactly E000 "b", while an IT-aware
disassembler renders it "beq". Both spellings can
be correct contextual descriptions of the same
machine halfword. Neither changes the original
instruction's legal encoding class.

The source assembly, object and decoder output
digests and the independent full E000..E7FF
decode check are retained in the companion
unconditional-branch audit receipt.

MACHINE-STATE PREMISES.

Execution is modeled under a symbolic ARMv7-A
unprivileged application process in T32 state.
Assume the machine code resides in an executable
virtual-memory mapping with legal T32 entry boundaries,
and no interrupt, external memory mutation or
unrelated fault intervenes in the permitted steps.

The starting register and flag values are declared
separately for each trace. Registers not declared
are UNKNOWN, not implicitly zero.
No data-memory reads or writes occur in the
permitted instructions described below.
The translation stops before executing an
ungranted SVC instruction at offset 0x000A.

FIRST EXECUTION, WITH R0 EQUAL TO ZERO.

Let the initial execution index be offset zero.
Let R0=0, R1 and R2 unknown, and N/Z/C/V unknown.

Step 1, offset 0x0000:
  halfword 2800, CMP R0,#0.
  The subtraction of zero from zero has result zero.
  Set N=0, Z=1, C=1 (no unsigned borrow), V=0.
  Preserve R0=0. No memory access.
  Advance index by two bytes to 0x0002.

Step 2, offset 0x0002:
  halfword BF08, T32 IT T1 with condition EQ,
  one following instruction slot.
  Establish active ITSTATE for the instruction
  beginning at offset 0x0004. IT does not
  update the four comparison flags.
  Advance index to 0x0004.

Step 3, offset 0x0004:
  halfword E000, B T2 immediate, operation 42.
  Its encoded eleven-bit displacement is zero,
  and the PC operand base equals offset 0x0008.
  The sole following IT condition is EQ, Z=1.
  The condition is true, so the B T2 takes
  its encoded target at offset 0x0008.
  The instruction is TWO bytes long but
  does not select the sequential index 0x0006.
  No register, LR, SP, flag or data memory
  value is altered. The IT slot is consumed.

  Therefore halfword 2103 at offset 0x0006
  is NOT architecturally executed on this path.
  There is no basis for setting R1 to three.

Step 4, offset 0x0008:
  halfword 2204, MOVS R2,#4.
  Operation 53 replaces R2 with unsigned four.
  N=0, Z=0; preserve C=1, V=0.
  Next execution index is offset 0x000A.

Step 5, offset 0x000A:
  halfword DF03 is SVC T1, decoded as
  supervisor-call immediate three.
  It is not in the current closed 72-operation
  English Major C execution grant.
  STOP and report the OS/exception boundary,
  rather than claiming the user-space program
  performed a particular service or returned.

The visited instruction addresses, including
the final decoded-but-not-executed boundary, are:
  0x0000, 0x0002, 0x0004, 0x0008, 0x000A.

Known registers at the boundary:
  R0=0; R1 UNKNOWN; R2=4;
  N=0; Z=0; C=1; V=0.

SECOND EXECUTION, WITH R0 EQUAL TO SEVEN.

The SAME six halfwords are used at the
SAME original addresses. Only the initial
machine state differs.

Begin at offset zero with R0=7.
R1/R2 and initial flags are unknown.

Step 1, CMP R0,#0, produces 7-0.
  Set N=0, Z=0, C=1, V=0.
  Continue to offset 0x0002.

Step 2, BF08, establishes one-slot IT EQ,
  and continues to offset 0x0004.

Step 3, E000 is the SAME B T2 encoding.
  Its normal effect would branch to
  offset 0x0008 from its PC operand base.
  But the active IT condition EQ is FALSE
  because Z is zero.
  Therefore do NOT perform the branch.
  Advance across this TWO-byte instruction
  to ordinary sequential offset 0x0006.
  Complete the final IT slot and leave
  the IT block. Preserve flags.

Step 4, offset 0x0006, MOVS R1,#3,
  sets R1=3; N=0, Z=0; C=1,V=0.
  Continue to offset 0x0008.

Step 5, offset 0x0008, MOVS R2,#4,
  sets R2=4; N=0, Z=0; C=1,V=0.
  Continue to offset 0x000A.

Step 6, offset 0x000A, encounter DF03,
  SVC T1; STOP as ungranted.
  No syscall effect is claimed.

The visited instruction addresses are:
  0x0000, 0x0002, 0x0004,
  0x0006, 0x0008, 0x000A.

Known registers at the boundary:
  R0=7; R1=3; R2=4;
  N=0; Z=0; C=1; V=0.

WHAT THIS EXAMPLE DEMONSTRATES.

A 16-bit B T2 is syntactically unconditional
because the halfword has no condition-code field.
But its architectural data and branch effects
can be conditional when it is legally placed
in the FINAL slot of a preceding T32 IT block.

The effect is not provided by the branch predictor,
the cache, the numerical branch target,
or some hidden data dependency.
It is provided by the ITSTATE previously
established by an ACTUAL IT machine instruction.

By contrast, a T1 conditional B from D000..DDFF
carries a condition in its own halfword and is
NOT permitted anywhere inside an IT block.
One cannot replace E000 in this example with
D000 while preserving the same native legal
IT sequence. The resulting program would be
architecturally UNPREDICTABLE.

The same program-counter address 0x0008 is reached
on both paths, but the paths have different
register effects, because one path skips the
ordinary MOVS at offset 0x0006.

A source translation which merely outputs
R2=4 has omitted essential information:
whether R1 was assigned, where the next
instruction address came from, and what
made the branch conditional.

A direct conditional branch around a register
assignment might implement a comparable
high-level computation, but it would be DIFFERENT
actual machine code and would need its own
translation and evidence. That alternative
belongs in a decision record, not in a false
claim about the observed E000 halfword.

SECONDARY BOUNDARY: A BREAKPOINT IN A FALSE IT SLOT.

A future or external instruction stream might
contain BF08 followed by BE01, with Z=0.
The BF08 would declare IT EQ, whose condition
is false for the following slot.
However BE01 is BKPT T1, an always-unconditional
debug-event instruction even inside an IT block.
It is NOT a conditionally skipped register write
or a conditionally skipped short B.

The initial English Major C profile does
not grant BKPT. It must classify BE01 as a
breakpoint event and STOP, regardless of
the false EQ condition, rather than silently
turning the ungranted instruction into a NOP.

This separate two-halfword counterexample
was not executed on a processor and does
not belong to the main six-halfword diagnostic.
Its byte meanings are defined in the
"Every breakpoint halfword and its
conditional-execution exception.english"
architecture file. The entire BE00..BEFF
range is enumerated in a data-only TSV.

CONSIDERED ALTERNATIVES RETAINED.

(1) Encode T2 B in last slot of IT, as
    the actual inspected diagnostic does.
    Legal when all stated restrictions hold.

(2) Use T1 conditional B outside IT.
    This requires changing machine bytes,
    branch-immediate range and surrounding
    control flow. Not an explanation of
    the original E000 bit pattern.

(3) Use a wide T32 conditional B T3
    after removing IT. Reach and encoding
    differ. Must verify exact availability.

(4) Use only arithmetic mask construction
    and avoid a branch. This may preserve
    some output values but not a byte-exact
    translation or necessarily identical flags.

(5) Use a predicated MOV rather than
    a predicated branch. This may work
    for short conditional data updates,
    but would require a changed input program.

(6) Rely on branch prediction to
    increase speed. Physical hardware
    details and measured distributions
    are missing. No universal claim is made.

(7) Attempt to skip an ungranted BKPT
    merely by making its IT condition false.
    Rejected because BKPT is always unconditional.
    Only actual control flow which does not
    select BKPT can avoid its event.

(8) Interpret DF03 by guessing the
    Linux/Android syscall table. Rejected;
    the source/target ABI and exception
    handler have not been declared.

FUTURE REQUIRED ACCEPTANCE.

Check exact six source halfwords against
the diagnostic object's native .text bytes.
Check T2 immediate range, instruction length,
PC-relative target, and IT last-slot placement.
Run both flag paths through a genuinely accepted
English Major C semantic executor once available,
including preserved unknown-register values.
Confirm no phantom execution at skipped 0x0006.
Confirm halt without executing ungranted DF03.
Reject substitution of T1 conditional B inside IT.
Reject a claim that false IT suppresses BKPT.
Reject every implication that source/disassembly
alone establishes physical CPU execution.

END OF SOURCE-DERIVED B T2 / IT CONTRAST.
